\section{Introduction}\label{sec:egraph-intro}

\emph{Equality saturation} is a recently developed optimization technique that uses \emph{equality graphs} (e-graphs) to represent and exploit equivalences between expressions. Unlike traditional compiler optimization passes, which apply transformations sequentially and irreversibly, equality saturation maintains a compact representation of all equivalent forms of an expression simultaneously and extracts the optimal form at the end.

The e-graph data structure, originally developed in the theorem proving community~\cite{nelson1980fast} (Nelson and Oppen, 1980), provides an efficient representation for equivalence classes of expressions. When combined with equality saturation, e-graphs enable a new approach to compiler optimization that is more composable, more thorough, and often more effective than traditional pass-based optimization.

This chapter develops the theory and practice of e-graphs and equality saturation, covering the core data structure, the saturation algorithm, rewrite rules, extraction, cost models, and modern implementations like \texttt{egg} and \texttt{Ruler}.

\section{E-Graphs: Definition and Operations}\label{sec:egraph-definition}

\subsection{Definition}\label{sec:egraph-def}

\begin{definition}[E-Graph]\label{def:egraph}
An \textbf{e-graph} is a data structure that maintains a set of \textbf{e-classes} (equivalence classes of expressions). Each e-class contains a set of \textbf{e-nodes}, where each e-node represents an expression (an operator applied to child e-classes). The e-graph maintains the invariant that if two e-nodes have the same operator and the same children (modulo e-class equality), they are in the same e-class.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[E-Class]\label{def:e-class}
An \textbf{e-class} is an equivalence class of e-nodes. All e-nodes in the same e-class represent equivalent expressions. E-classes are identified by unique identifiers and can be merged.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[E-Node]\label{def:e-node}
An \textbf{e-node} is a pair $(f, [c_1, c_2, \ldots, c_k])$ where $f$ is an operator (function symbol) and $c_1, \ldots, c_k$ are e-class identifiers representing the children.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Core Operations}\label{sec:egraph-operations}

The e-graph supports three fundamental operations:

\begin{definition}[E-Graph Operations]\label{def:egraph-ops}
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Add}: Add an expression to the e-graph, creating e-nodes and e-classes as needed. Returns the e-class of the expression.
  \item \textbf{Merge}: Merge two e-classes, making them equivalent. All e-nodes in both classes are now in the same equivalence class.
  \item \textbf{Rebuild}: Restore the e-graph invariants after a sequence of merges. This involves recomputing the hash-consing (ensuring structurally identical e-nodes are in the same class) and propagating equivalences.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{E-Graph Add Operation}
\label{alg:egraph-add}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require E-graph $G$, expression $e$
\Ensure E-class of $e$ in $G$
\If{$e$ is a variable $x$}
  \State Create e-node $(x, [])$
  \State Create new e-class $c$ containing this e-node
  \State \Return $c$
\ElsIf{$e$ is $f(e_1, \ldots, e_k)$}
  \State $c_i \gets \textsc{Add}(G, e_i)$ for each $i$
  \State Create e-node $n = (f, [c_1, \ldots, c_k])$
  \If{$n$ already exists in $G$}
    \State \Return e-class of existing $n$
  \Else
    \State Create new e-class $c$ containing $n$
    \State \Return $c$
  \EndIf
\EndIf
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{E-Graph Merge Operation}
\label{alg:egraph-merge}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require E-graph $G$, e-classes $c_1, c_2$
\Ensure $c_1$ and $c_2$ are merged
\State $c_1' \gets \textsc{Find}(c_1)$ \Comment{Find canonical representative}
\State $c_2' \gets \textsc{Find}(c_2)$
\If{$c_1' \neq c_2'$}
  \State Union $c_1'$ and $c_2'$ (union-find)
  \State Mark $G$ as needing rebuild
\EndIf
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{The Rebuild Algorithm}\label{sec:rebuild}

The \emph{rebuild} operation is the most complex part of the e-graph. After merges, the e-graph may have e-nodes that are structurally identical but in different e-classes. Rebuild restores the invariant by:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Recomputing the hash-consing table (mapping e-node structures to e-classes).
  \item Merging e-classes that now contain identical e-nodes.
  \item Propagating equivalences: if two e-classes are merged, all parent e-nodes that reference them must be updated.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{E-Graph Rebuild}
\label{alg:egraph-rebuild}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require E-graph $G$ (dirty, after merges)
\Ensure $G$ satisfies all invariants
\While{$G$ has pending changes}
  \ForAll{e-classes $c$ that were modified}
    \ForAll{e-nodes $n$ in $c$}
      \State Update $n$'s children to canonical representatives
      \State Look up $n$ in hash-consing table
      \If{equivalent e-node $n'$ exists}
        \State Merge $c$ with e-class of $n'$
      \Else
        \State Insert $n$ into hash-consing table
      \EndIf
    \EndFor
  \EndFor
\EndWhile
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\section{Equality Saturation Algorithm}\label{sec:equality-saturation}

\subsection{Overview}\label{sec:es-overview}

\emph{Equality saturation} applies a set of rewrite rules to an e-graph until no more rules apply (a fixedpoint is reached). The e-graph accumulates all equivalent forms of the original expression, and the best form is extracted at the end.

\begin{definition}[Equality Saturation]\label{def:equality-saturation}
\textbf{Equality saturation} is the process of:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Adding the original expression to the e-graph.
  \item Repeatedly applying rewrite rules: for each rule $l \to r$, find all matches of $l$ in the e-graph and add $r$ (merged with $l$).
  \item Continue until no more rules apply or a resource limit is reached.
  \item Extract the best (lowest-cost) expression from the e-graph.
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{Equality Saturation}
\label{alg:equality-saturation}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require Expression $e$, rewrite rules $R$, cost function $\mathit{cost}$
\Ensure Optimized expression $e'$
\State $G \gets$ new e-graph
\State $c \gets \textsc{Add}(G, e)$
\Repeat
  \State $\mathit{changed} \gets \text{false}$
  \ForAll{rules $l \to r \in R$}
    \ForAll{matches of $l$ in $G$}
      \State $c_l \gets$ e-class of match
      \State $c_r \gets \textsc{Add}(G, r[\text{matching substitution}])$
      \State $\textsc{Merge}(G, c_l, c_r)$
      \State $\mathit{changed} \gets \text{true}$
    \EndFor
  \EndFor
  \State $\textsc{Rebuild}(G)$
\Until{$\neg \mathit{changed}$ or resource limit reached}
\State \Return $\textsc{Extract}(G, c, \mathit{cost})$
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{Advantages Over Traditional Optimization}\label{sec:es-advantages}

Equality saturation has several advantages over traditional sequential optimization passes:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Order independence}: Rules can be applied in any order; the result is the same (up to the fixedpoint). No need to worry about pass ordering.
  \item \textbf{Commute and associate}: Rules like $a + b = b + a$ and $(a + b) + c = a + (b + c)$ are naturally handled because all equivalent forms coexist in the e-graph.
  \item \textbf{Phase ordering}: Traditional optimization suffers from the ``phase ordering problem'' (pass A enables pass B but disables pass C). Equality saturation avoids this by applying all rules simultaneously.
\end{itemize}

\section{Rewrite Rules and Extraction}\label{sec:rewrite-extraction}

\subsection{Rewrite Rules}\label{sec:rewrite-rules}

\begin{definition}[Rewrite Rule]\label{def:rewrite-rule}
A \textbf{rewrite rule} is a pair $(l, r)$ where $l$ and $r$ are expressions with variables. The rule states that any substitution instance of $l$ is equivalent to the corresponding instance of $r$.
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[Algebraic Rewrite Rules]\label{ex:rewrite-rules}
\begin{verbatim}
  x + 0         → x                    (additive identity)
  x * 1         → x                    (multiplicative identity)
  x * 0         → 0                    (annihilation)
  x + x         → x * 2               (strength reduction)
  (x + y) + z   → x + (y + z)         (associativity)
  x + y         → y + x               (commutativity)
  x - x         → 0                   (subtraction self)
  x / x         → 1                   (division self, x ≠ 0)
\end{verbatim}
\end{example}

\subsection{Pattern Matching in E-Graphs}\label{sec:pattern-matching-egraph}

Matching a pattern $l$ in an e-graph requires finding all e-nodes that match $l$ under some substitution. This is more complex than tree matching because e-graphs represent equivalence classes.

\begin{definition}[E-Graph Matching]\label{def:egraph-matching}
A \textbf{match} of pattern $l$ in e-graph $G$ is a substitution $\sigma$ mapping variables in $l$ to e-classes in $G$ such that $l\sigma$ is represented in $G$ (i.e., the e-node for $l\sigma$ exists and its e-class is found).
\end{definition}

\section{Cost Models for Extraction}\label{sec:cost-models}

\subsection{The Extraction Problem}\label{sec:extraction-problem}

After equality saturation, the e-graph contains many equivalent forms of the original expression. \emph{Extraction} selects the best (lowest-cost) form.

\begin{definition}[Extraction]\label{def:extraction}
\textbf{Extraction} finds the expression $e'$ in the e-class $c$ that minimizes $\mathit{cost}(e')$, where $\mathit{cost}$ is a cost function assigning a numeric cost to each expression.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Cost Function]\label{def:cost-function}
A \textbf{cost function} $\mathit{cost} : \mathit{Expr} \to \mathbb{N}$ assigns a cost to each expression. Common cost models:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Size}: Number of nodes in the expression tree.
  \item \textbf{Instruction cost}: Sum of instruction latencies.
  \item \textbf{Custom}: Domain-specific cost (e.g., preferring vectorized operations).
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\subsection{Extraction Algorithm}\label{sec:extraction-algorithm}

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{E-Graph Extraction}
\label{alg:extraction}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require E-graph $G$, root e-class $c$, cost function $\mathit{cost}$
\Ensure Lowest-cost expression in e-class $c$
\ForAll{e-classes $c'$ in $G$ (bottom-up)}
  \State $\mathit{best}[c'] \gets \min_{n \in c'} (\mathit{cost}(n.\mathit{op}, \mathit{best}[n.\mathit{children}]))$
\EndFor
\State \Return expression corresponding to $\mathit{best}[c]$
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\begin{theorem}[Extraction Correctness]\label{thm:extraction-correct}
If the cost function is \emph{monotone} (replacing a subexpression with a cheaper equivalent never increases the total cost), then the extraction algorithm finds the globally optimal expression.
\end{theorem}

\subsection{Non-Monotone Cost Functions}\label{sec:non-monotone}

When the cost function is not monotone (e.g., when instruction scheduling constraints make the cost of a subexpression depend on its context), extraction becomes NP-hard. In practice, the bottom-up extraction provides a good approximation.

\section{Applications to Compiler Optimization}\label{sec:egraph-applications}

\subsection{Peephole Optimization}\label{sec:egraph-peephole}

E-graphs can replace traditional peephole optimization by encoding all peephole rules as rewrite rules and running equality saturation on each basic block.

\begin{example}[Peephole via E-Graph]\label{ex:egraph-peephole}
\begin{verbatim}
  // Rewrite rules:
  x + 0 → x
  x * 1 → x
  x << 0 → x
  x - x → 0
  
  // Input: (a + 0) * 1 - (a + 0)
  // After saturation: e-graph contains both the original and "0"
  // Extraction: "0" (cost 1 < cost of original)
\end{verbatim}
\end{example}

\subsection{Instruction Selection}\label{sec:egraph-isel}

E-graphs can encode instruction selection patterns as rewrite rules, combining pattern matching and optimization in a single framework.

\subsection{Superoptimization}\label{sec:egraph-superopt}

\emph{Superoptimization} searches for the optimal instruction sequence for a given computation. E-graphs enable superoptimization by exploring all equivalent instruction sequences simultaneously.

\section{egg, Ruler, and Modern Implementations}\label{sec:modern-implementations}

\subsection{egg}\label{sec:egg}

\emph{egg}~\cite{willsey2021egg} is a fast, extensible e-graph library written in Rust. It provides:
\begin{itemize}
  \item A generic e-graph implementation parameterized by the language (operators and their arities).
  \item A pattern matching engine for finding matches in e-graphs.
  \item An extraction algorithm with configurable cost functions.
  \item \emph{Equality saturation driver} that applies rules until convergence.
\end{itemize}

\begin{definition}[egg Architecture]\label{def:egg-arch}
\textbf{egg} is built on the \texttt{Language} trait, which defines:
\begin{itemize}
  \item The set of operators (enum variants).
  \item The arity of each operator.
  \item Hashing and equality for e-nodes.
\end{itemize}
Users define their language by implementing this trait, and egg provides the e-graph, matching, and saturation infrastructure.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Ruler}\label{sec:ruler}

\emph{Ruler}~\cite{nandi2021ruler} is a tool that automatically discovers rewrite rules using e-graphs. Given a set of expressions (e.g., all expressions up to a certain size), Ruler uses equality saturation to find equivalences and then extracts a minimal set of rewrite rules that generate all discovered equivalences.

\begin{definition}[Rule Discovery]\label{def:rule-discovery}
\textbf{Rule discovery} is the process of finding a set of rewrite rules $R$ such that:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $R$ is \emph{sound}: every equivalence derivable from $R$ is a true equivalence.
  \item $R$ is \emph{complete} (for a given expression set): every equivalence in the expression set is derivable from $R$.
  \item $R$ is \emph{minimal}: no rule in $R$ is derivable from the other rules.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\subsection{Applications Beyond Compilers}\label{sec:beyond-compilers}

E-graphs and equality saturation have applications beyond compiler optimization:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Program synthesis}: Search for programs that satisfy a specification by exploring equivalent programs.
  \item \textbf{Theorem proving}: Maintain equivalence classes of terms during proof search.
  \item \textbf{Computer algebra}: Simplify and transform mathematical expressions.
  \item \textbf{Quantum compilation}: Optimize quantum circuits by exploring equivalent gate sequences.
\end{itemize}

